# Sensor Changelog: Webcam Bring-Up

Date: 2026-05-27
Branch: Sun/Webcamera-1

Historical note: the shell scripts described below were later retired. Current
bring-up uses `ros2 launch jmu_lunabotics master.launch.py`.

## Summary

Set up the first webcam pipeline for the ROS 2 Jazzy workspace. The new tooling
can install camera dependencies, publish webcam frames into ROS, visualize the
raw image stream, and launch the publisher/viewer together.

## Bootstrap Updates

Updated `scripts/bootstrap_dev.sh` so bootstrap installs camera and visualization
tools:

```bash
ros-jazzy-usb-cam
ros-jazzy-v4l2-camera
ros-jazzy-rqt-image-view
```

The bootstrap progress labels were updated from `1/7` to `1/8` because camera
setup is now its own install step.

## New Camera Publisher

Created:

```text
sensor/webcam/publish_camera.sh
```

This script publishes webcam data into ROS. By default, it uses:

```bash
v4l2_camera
```

from:

```text
/dev/video0
```

and publishes:

```text
/camera/image_raw
/camera/camera_info
```

The camera device can be changed with:

```bash
VIDEO_DEVICE=/dev/video2 sensor/webcam/publish_camera.sh
```

The driver can be switched to `usb_cam` with:

```bash
WEBCAM_DRIVER=usb_cam sensor/webcam/publish_camera.sh
```

## New Image Viewer

Created:

```text
sensor/webcam/view_image_raw.sh
```

This script opens:

```text
/camera/image_raw
```

using `rqt_image_view`.

The topic can be changed with:

```bash
IMAGE_TOPIC=/some/other/image_raw sensor/webcam/view_image_raw.sh
```

## New Sensor Launcher

Created:

```text
launch/launch_sensors.sh
```

This script starts the camera publisher, waits until `/camera/image_raw` exists,
then opens the image viewer. When the viewer exits or the launcher is stopped,
it cleans up the camera publisher process.

At that point, the full flow was launched with:

```bash
~/dev_ws/src/jmu-lunabotics/launch/launch_sensors.sh
```

## Technical Details

The webcam appears to Linux as a video device, usually:

```text
/dev/video0
```

A ROS camera driver reads frames from that device and republishes them as ROS
topics.

The main image stream is:

```text
/camera/image_raw
```

with message type:

```text
sensor_msgs/msg/Image
```

This contains the raw camera frame data. AprilTag detection nodes will later
subscribe to this topic.

The camera metadata topic is:

```text
/camera/camera_info
```

with message type:

```text
sensor_msgs/msg/CameraInfo
```

This carries camera calibration information such as focal length, optical
center, distortion coefficients, and frame geometry. For AprilTag pose
estimation, this topic should eventually come from a calibrated camera rather
than default camera info.

The default publisher path uses:

```bash
ros2 run v4l2_camera v4l2_camera_node
```

The script remaps the driver's native topics into the project convention:

```text
/camera/image_raw
/camera/camera_info
```
